# ============================================================================
# FlowAblation / laminar boundary-layer heat flux on a flat plate, Mach 2  (Hydro2, ablation.on = 1)
#
# The heat flux that drives ablation in a stream.  Gas (rho 1, p 1, T_inf 1; gamma 1.4, cp 3.5,
# R = 1, mu 2e-4, Pr 0.72) enters at Mach 2 (U = 2.3664) over a flat no-slip wall along y = 0
# (the embedded solid fills y < 0), isothermal at T_w = T_inf; the leading edge is the inflow
# boundary.  Re_L = rho U L/mu = 11832.  Q* is set huge (1e9) so the wall does not move: the test
# is the wall heat flux, against the compressible laminar flat-plate solution
#     q_w(x) = St rho U cp (T_aw - T_w),   St = 0.332 sqrt(C*) Pr^(-2/3) / sqrt(Re_x),
#     T_aw = T_inf (1 + sqrt(Pr) (gamma - 1)/2 M^2) = 1.679,
#     C* = rho* mu*/(rho_e mu_e) at Eckert's reference temperature T* = T_e + 0.5 (T_w - T_e) + 0.22 (T_aw - T_e)
# (constant mu here, so C* = T_e/T* = 0.870).  The reference-temperature method is good to a few
# per cent; near the inlet the weak leading-edge shock and the coarse resolution of the thin
# layer are not covered by it.  400 x 200 cells (dx = 0.0025; the layer is ~18 cells thick at x = 1).
#
# Run (from bin/): mpirun -np 8 ./hydro2-2d-g++ ../tests/FlowAblation/input_2D_FlatPlate plot_file=<dir>/output
# Check:           python3 ../tests/FlowAblation/reference/flat_plate_check.py <dir>
# ============================================================================
